{
  "dom_id": "step-q13339",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Write $3y+3x+3=0$ in standard form.",
  "worked_solution": "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+3x+3 & =0 \\\\ 3x+3y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, $a,b,$ and $c$ have a common factor of $3.$ So, we divide by $3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+3y & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{3x+3y}{3} & =\\frac{-3}{3} \\\\ x+y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x+y=-1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
